## API Guide

### A server and a client

```c
#include <mulle-socket/mulle-socket.h>

unsigned int     port;
mulle_socket_t   listener;
mulle_socket_t   connection;

/* 0 for the port asks the platform for one, and reports back which */
listener = mulle_socket_listen( "127.0.0.1", 0, &port);
if( listener == MULLE_SOCKET_INVALID)
   return( -1);

/* the next client, with TCP_NODELAY already set on it */
connection = mulle_socket_accept( listener);
if( connection == MULLE_SOCKET_INVALID)
   return( -1);

if( mulle_socket_write_all( connection, "+PONG\r\n", 7))
   return( -1);

mulle_socket_close( connection);
mulle_socket_close( listener);
```

```c
mulle_socket_t   client;

client = mulle_socket_connect( "127.0.0.1", port);
if( client == MULLE_SOCKET_INVALID)
{
   fprintf( stderr, "cannot connect: %s\n", strerror( mulle_socket_error()));
   return( -1);
}
```

Everything is blocking. `mulle_socket_find_free_port()` is the racy helper for
"a port for a server I am about to start": it binds port 0, reads back what it
got, and closes again, so the port is free when it returns rather than reserved.

### The three promises

1. **`SIGPIPE` never reaches your process.** `MSG_NOSIGNAL` on Linux,
   `SO_NOSIGPIPE` on the BSDs and macOS, and neither on Windows because there is
   nothing to suppress. A write to a peer that has gone gives `-1` and `EPIPE`
   on all three. A platform with neither flag fails to *build* rather than
   killing your process at the worst moment.

2. **`EINTR` is retried inside.** Every call that can be interrupted retries
   it, so callers never write that loop. Nothing else is retried: `accept` on a
   closed listener, a refused `connect` and a reset connection are all reported,
   with `mulle_socket_error()` for the reason.

3. **`mulle_socket_error()` is an `errno` value on every platform.** Winsock's
   codes that have a POSIX twin are translated (`WSAEWOULDBLOCK` to `EAGAIN`,
   `WSAECONNREFUSED` to `ECONNREFUSED`, `WSAESHUTDOWN` to `EPIPE`, and so on);
   the rest arrive as `EIO`, which is the honest answer for "it failed in a way
   this API has no name for". `strerror()` on the result is meaningful.

### Timeouts, and where they stop

`mulle_socket_set_read_timeout()` and `mulle_socket_set_write_timeout()` bound a
blocking read or write, in seconds, and an expiry arrives as `ETIMEDOUT`
on every platform — POSIX says `EAGAIN` for one and Winsock says `WSAETIMEDOUT`
for the other, and a caller should not have to know which. `0` means wait
forever, which is also the default.

Two boundaries are the caller's, and both are measured rather than assumed:

- **`accept` is bounded on Linux only.** Linux's kernel applies the receive
timeout to `accept`; BSD, macOS and Winsock do not, so an accept on an idle
listener there waits however small the timeout is. A caller who needs one wants
`select()`. The library does not wrap that, because wrapping it would change
what `accept` means on two platforms without the socket option that is supposed
to mean it.
- **`connect` is bounded nowhere.** No socket option expresses it; bounding a
connect means a non-blocking connect plus a readiness wait, and a half-open
socket to clean up when it expires.

`probe/timeout-probe.c` is what established all of this by hanging on exactly
the platforms that ignore the option, and it is kept — with its answers written
down in `probe/README.md` — for the next person who wonders whether the header
is right. There is still no
non-blocking mode: a call without a timeout can block indefinitely, and a caller
who needs a deadline for one of the two unbounded calls uses the platform's own
readiness wait on a `mulle_socket_t` — which is exactly what that call is for.
- **No peer address from `accept`.** Nothing in the tree needed one, and
  inventing a `getpeername` wrapper means deciding what to report when the peer
  has already vanished.
- **No unix domain sockets on Windows.** Windows has had `AF_UNIX` since 10
  1803, but whether `<afunix.h>` and `struct sockaddr_un` are there depends on
  the SDK in a way this library cannot paper over, and nothing here needed one.
  They are offered on POSIX, where `MULLE_SOCKET_HAVE_UNIX` is defined.
- **No `SO_REUSEADDR` on Windows**, on purpose and not as an omission — see the
  table above and `mulle_socket_set_reuseaddr()`, which is a no-op there that
  returns success so the call needs no platform guard.

### Where the callers live

`mulle-core-demo`'s miniredis is the first customer: its listener and accept
loop, its client `connect`, its port picking and its unix socket connect all go
through this library, which is what let its TCP transport and its four TCP
fixtures run on Windows. The `TCP_NODELAY` measurement that justifies setting it
on `accept` and `connect` — 16 pipelined 4 KB requests answering their last reply
in 34 ms instead of 0.15 ms — is recorded in `mulle-socket.h`, next to the calls
that set it.

### Verification

`test/10-api/socket.c` exercises this whole header in one process — a listener, a
client that connects to it, a 4 KB binary round trip, `shutdown` and the `EPIPE`
that follows, a refused connection, a rebind of the same port, `close` on
`MULLE_SOCKET_INVALID`, and the three timeouts — and `mulle-sde test run` is what
runs it. Its record is per platform: `socket.stdout` for Linux, and
`socket.stdout.darwin` and `socket.stdout.windows` where the differences below
are not cosmetic. Anything that varies run to run (ports, elapsed times) is
behind `--verbose` instead, so the records hold:

| | Linux | macOS | Windows (wine) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `test/10-api/socket.c` | **44 ok** | **41 ok** | **41 ok** |
| refused connection reads as | `ECONNREFUSED` (111) | `ECONNREFUSED` (61) | `ECONNREFUSED` (107, translated) |
| write after shutdown reads as | `EPIPE` (32) | `EPIPE` (32) | `EPIPE` (32, translated from `WSAESHUTDOWN`) |
| rebind after a connection, with no `SO_REUSEADDR` | works (it was set) | works (it was set) | works (it was not needed) |
| a 0.25 s read timeout | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.25s | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.25s | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.25s |
| a 0.25 s write timeout | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.77s | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.50s | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.25s |
| a 0.3 s accept timeout | `ETIMEDOUT` @0.30s | *not bounded by the option* | *not bounded by the option* |

The platform with the higher check count is the one where `accept` is bounded;
the other two say so in a line instead of asserting it. The write timeout differs
in wall-clock the other way round, and for a reason worth keeping: an expiring
POSIX send returns the bytes it did move, so `write_all` earns several timeouts'
worth of progress before it gives up, while Windows fails the send outright. On
macOS one megabyte made it through, on Linux two, on Windows none — the check
asserts the error and the bound, never the byte count.

The rebind check is the one that says the `SO_REUSEADDR` asymmetry is right: it
passes on all three, with the option set where it means "rebind" and not set
where it means "hijack".
